Got it — so the stack is: React UI → Claude Code (running locally via your Orbital wrapper or similar) → response streams back. No backend in the middle. That actually simplifies the brief significantly because the "server orchestration" phase collapses into the Claude Code session itself.
Here's the mental shift: Claude Code is your backend. It already has tool execution, file access, MCP, the agent loop. Your React UI just needs to (a) send prompts in, (b) parse the streamed response, (c) render UI tool blocks as components. The "data tools" you'd normally write server-side are already there — Claude Code can read files, query databases via MCP, run scripts. You only need to add UI tools, and those don't actually execute anything — they're just structured output the model emits that your React app interprets.
The architecture, revised for your stack
┌──────────────┐    stdin/socket     ┌─────────────────┐
│  React UI    │ ──────────────────▶ │  Claude Code    │
│  (Orbital)   │ ◀── stream ──────── │  (local agent)  │
│              │                     │  + MCP servers  │
│  Renderer +  │                     │  + file tools   │
│  Registry    │                     └─────────────────┘
└──────────────┘
The trick is: UI tools aren't real tools that Claude Code executes. They're a convention — you tell Claude in the system prompt "when you want to show a chart, output a fenced code block tagged ui:chart with this JSON shape." Your React app parses those blocks out of the stream and renders components. No tool registration needed on the Claude Code side.
This is cleaner than it sounds. Two patterns work:
Pattern A — Fenced JSON blocks (simplest)
Claude emits markdown like:
Here's the failure trend:

```````ui:chart
{ "type": "line", "data": [...], "x": "date", "y": "failures" }
```````

And the top offenders:

```````ui:table
{ "columns": [...], "rows": [...] }
```````
Your React renderer splits the stream on fenced blocks, parses ui:* ones as components, renders the rest as markdown. Works with any model, any transport.
Pattern B — Custom XML tags
Same idea, different syntax: <ui-chart>...</ui-chart>. Slightly easier to stream-parse (you can render partial blocks before the closing tag arrives). Claude is good at honoring XML structure.
I'd go with Pattern A — Claude Code already streams markdown, and fenced code blocks are something the model is trained to emit reliably.
