markdown# Generative UI Layer for Orbital — Build Brief

## Stack reality
- React + Vite frontend (Orbital, single-file npm package)
- Talks directly to Claude Code via Express/Socket.io bridge
- NO backend service, NO Spring Boot, NO Next.js
- Claude Code is the agent — it already has file/MCP/bash tools
- We are NOT registering new tools with Claude Code. We are adding a
  RENDERING CONVENTION the model follows in its text output.

## Goal
When Claude Code's response contains specially-tagged JSON blocks,
render them as React components instead of raw text. Everything else
renders as markdown.

## Phase 1 — Primitive components (/src/generative/primitives/)
Pure presentational, no data fetching, strict prop types:

  KpiCard, DataTable (TanStack), Chart (Recharts), Timeline,
  Grid (layout), Markdown (fallback)

Each accepts the JSON payload from a ui:* block as its props.

## Phase 2 — The convention (system prompt addendum)
Add to the system prompt sent to Claude Code on session start:

  "When presenting structured data, emit a fenced code block with
   one of these language tags instead of raw markdown tables:

```````ui:kpi      → { label, value, delta?, trend? }
``````ui:table    → { columns: [{key,label,type?}], rows: [...] }
`````ui:chart    → { type, data, x, y, series? }
````ui:timeline → { events: [{ts,label,status?}] }
```ui:grid     → { columns: 1|2|3, blocks: [<nested ui blocks>] }

   Use them whenever the data is tabular, numeric, time-series, or
   comparative. Use regular markdown for prose, code, and explanation."

This addendum lives in /src/generative/system-prompt.ts and is
injected by the Orbital socket bridge on every new session.

## Phase 3 — Stream parser (/src/generative/parser.ts)
As Claude Code streams markdown chunks back over the socket:
  - buffer chunks
  - detect ```ui:<kind>\n...\n``` regions
  - emit a sequence of {type:'markdown', content} | {type:'ui', kind, payload}
    blocks to the renderer
  - handle partial blocks (closing fence not yet arrived) by holding
    the buffer until complete, then emitting

Streaming requirement: markdown blocks render as they arrive; ui
blocks render only once the closing ``` is seen and JSON parses.

## Phase 4 — Renderer (/src/generative/Renderer.tsx)
Single component, takes the parsed block array, maps each block to a
component. Unknown ui:kind values render <UnknownBlock payload={...} />
showing the raw JSON in a collapsible panel — never throw.

## Acceptance tests
- "show batch failure rate last 7 days" → response contains exactly
  one ```ui:chart block, renders Chart component, no raw JSON visible.
- "summarize yesterday's runs" → response contains ```ui:grid wrapping
  KpiCards + a DataTable.
- Malformed JSON inside a ui:* block → UnknownBlock renders, app does
  not crash, error visible to user.
- Plain conversational reply ("hi") → renders as markdown, no UI blocks.
- Streaming: markdown text appears progressively; ui blocks appear
  atomically once their closing fence arrives.

## Anti-goals (reject any code that does these)
- Trying to register tools with Claude Code via MCP for rendering
  (we don't need to — text convention is sufficient)
- Hardcoding a "BatchFailureDashboard" or any one-off report component
- Letting the model emit raw HTML or JSX strings
- Using dangerouslySetInnerHTML
- Fetching any data inside a primitive component
- Eval'ing any part of the model output
```

A few things worth flagging to Copilot specifically, because they're easy to get wrong:

**The system prompt addendum is the entire spec.** If the model doesn't reliably emit `ui:chart` blocks, no amount of frontend work helps. Spend time on the wording — give 2-3 examples in the prompt itself. "Here's good output: [example]. Here's bad output: [example]." Claude follows examples better than rules.

**Stream parsing is the actual hard part.** Naive regex-on-every-chunk breaks because chunks split mid-fence. Buffer until you see a complete `\n```\n`, then emit. Markdown chunks between fences flush immediately. There's a known pattern for this — ask Copilot to use a state machine (`OUTSIDE_FENCE` / `INSIDE_FENCE` / `READING_LANG_TAG`) rather than regex.

**Keep the registry tiny at first.** Six primitives is already the upper end. The temptation will be to add `ui:diff`, `ui:logs`, `ui:filetree` immediately. Resist — add them only when a real prompt fails without them. Each new primitive is a new contract Claude has to remember.

One bonus this approach gives you that the tool-call version doesn't: **it works with any model and any transport**. If you ever swap Claude Code for a raw API call, or add a Gemini fallback, the rendering layer doesn't change. The convention is just text.

